# The Freshness Receiver [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

> A value that stopped arriving looked exactly like a value that had not changed. David's Dradis rendered `contacts: 3 threats: 0` with no contacts present and a stalled FTL-ready indicator — frozen last-known values from a publisher that had died, presented as though current, beside a `throttle/*` that updated fine because its publisher was alive. Nothing anywhere expired anything. The declaration half of the contract had shipped and NOTHING CONSUMED IT.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-FRESH-INTENT] A System That Cannot Say "I Do Not Know" Will Lie Instead

- problem: `Namespace.value` returned `state_engine.current` unconditionally. There was no age counter anywhere in the tree, so the last value ever received was rendered forever, indistinguishable from a current one. This is not a bug in a widget; it is a property the bus lacked, so every consumer inherited it and no consumer could have fixed it locally.
- the-tell: the defect surfaced because ONE namespace kept working. `throttle/*` updated while `dradis/*` sat frozen, and the contrast is the only reason anyone noticed — a fleet-wide publisher outage would have produced a completely consistent, completely wrong display with no internal evidence of trouble at all.
- why-the-declaration-alone-was-worse-than-nothing: `refresh_interval`, `staleness_timeout` and `Cadence` shipped, were tested, and passed. A reader of the code — or of `spec/01` — would reasonably conclude freshness worked. A half-built mechanism that tests green is more dangerous than an absent one, because the green suppresses the suspicion that would otherwise prompt a look ([PRINCIPLE-MACHINE-GREEN-IS-NOT-GLASS-GREEN]).
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/namespace.py:Namespace.value
- verify: python/tests/test_freshness_receiver.py::test_dradis_contacts_goes_unknown_when_the_world_app_stops
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

### [CXC-FRESH-ASYMMETRY] Freshness Is A RECEIVER'S Judgement And Never A PUBLISHER'S

- rule: a publisher reports WHAT IT SENT. Only a receiver applies a freshness verdict. No outbound envelope ever carries this bus's own staleness opinion.
- forcing-case: found by mutation, not by design. `bus.publish_state` re-read `ns.value` to build the outbound message, which pushes an accepted value back through the expiry gate BEFORE transmitting it. For a quiet-by-construction namespace whose node was marked silent, that gate answers `unknown` — so the bus would have transmitted `unknown` for a value it held in its hand one line earlier, corrupting a healthy signal on the way out because of a verdict about an unrelated node.
- consequence: `publish_state` uses the value returned by the namespace, never a re-read. The distinction is not stylistic: a re-read makes the two halves of the contract observable from the wrong side.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/bus.py:ComposeIOBus.publish_state
- verify: python/tests/test_freshness_receiver.py::test_a_publisher_never_transmits_its_own_freshness_verdict
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

---

## 2. The Mechanism

### [CXC-FRESH-MONOTONIC] Age Is Measured On A MONOTONIC Clock, Never A Shared One

- rule: the receiver stamps a monotonic instant on every RECEIPT and computes age as `monotonic() - last_receipt`. `time.time()` appears nowhere in the freshness path.
- why-not-wall-clock: [D4] rejected wire timestamps on three grounds, and the decisive one is that THERE IS NO SHARED TIME BASE. `_bus/time` is one-way, one-second resolution, no delay compensation. Against CH32V003 parts on internal oscillators at about 1% (roughly 36 s/hour), the clock-offset error EXCEEDS the staleness being measured — so a wall-clock scheme would report fresh values as expired, consistently, and would do so in a way that survives testing on one machine.
- second-reason: a monotonic clock cannot be dragged backwards by NTP mid-flight, which would otherwise make an expired value spontaneously un-expire.
- injectable: the clock is a constructor argument. A freshness test built on real sleeps is slow, flaky, and — decisively — CANNOT ASSERT THE NEGATIVE CONTROL, because "still fresh at 2.999 s" is not a claim a wall-clock sleep can make honestly.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/namespace.py:Namespace._clock
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

### [CXC-FRESH-RECEIPT] The Age Resets On RECEIPT, Not On CHANGE

- rule: every publish stamps a receipt, INCLUDING one whose value is identical to the last.
- why: `Namespace.publish_state` notifies subscribers only when `new != old`. An age counter driven by the subscriber callback would never see a publisher faithfully restating a steady value — so it would expire a transmitter that is working perfectly, and it would do so ONLY for the steadiest signals in the fleet, which are exactly the ones an operator most expects to trust.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/namespace.py:Namespace.mark_received
- verify: python/tests/test_freshness_receiver.py::test_a_republish_of_the_SAME_value_refreshes_the_age
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

### [CXC-FRESH-LAZY] Expiry Is Computed At READ And Nothing Sweeps

- rule: there is no timer, no reaper and no background task. The verdict is computed inside the `value` property, by whoever asks.
- why: [D4] — "a timer-driven sweep means a stalled receiver demotes the whole world and blames the world". A blocked GUI thread must not manufacture a fleet-wide outage out of its own stall, and under lazy evaluation it structurally cannot: a receiver that is not reading is not judging.
- self-healing: nothing latches. When the publisher returns, the next receipt makes the next read FRESH with no reset step for anyone to forget.
- one-measurement-path: every reader — `get_state`, the transports, the monitors, all 52 widget `on_bus_state` implementations — arrives through the same property, so a stale value cannot leak out through a second accessor ([PRINCIPLE-ONE-MEASUREMENT-PATH]). `raw_value` is the single deliberate, named exception, for diagnostics that need the last thing actually received.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/namespace.py:Namespace.value
- verify: python/tests/test_freshness_receiver.py::test_expiry_is_lazy_and_reversible_when_the_publisher_returns
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

### [CXC-FRESH-VERDICT] Six Cases, Because A Boolean Cannot Say WHY

- case-fresh: received within its declared window.
- case-stale: declared a cadence, and nothing arrived within 3x it.
- case-wire-unknown: the publisher sent an explicit 0xFD — alive, and knowingly without a value.
- case-never-received: declared a cadence, and nothing has EVER arrived.
- case-node-silent: quiet-by-construction, and its node stopped beating.
- case-undeclared: no contract, so no verdict was ever authorised.
- timeout-formula: `max(3 * refresh_interval, refresh_interval + 0.1)`. The 3x is a J1939 CONVENTION and the spec must say convention rather than requirement; the floor stops a fast cadence producing a timeout inside its own jitter.
- why-not-a-boolean: the four failure cases call for different operator responses — a NEVER_RECEIVED namespace may simply be early in boot, a STALE one has a publisher that died, a NODE_SILENT one has a whole board gone. Collapsing them hands the glass one word where the operator needs four.
- ordering-is-load-bearing: WIRE_UNKNOWN precedes every age rule (an explicit 0xFD is a POSITIVE statement and must not be relabelled). UNDECLARED precedes anything time-based (no verdict is invented for a contract nobody made). NEVER_RECEIVED precedes STALE (there is no last value being disbelieved; there was never a first).
- undeclared-is-not-fresh: reporting UNDECLARED as FRESH is how a system claims confidence it never earned. It is reported as its own case and counted separately in `freshness_report`, so "nothing is wrong" can never be read off a display that means "nothing was checked".
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/freshness.py:Verdict
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

