## 3. The Trap: Quiet By Design Is Not Dead

### [CXC-FRESH-QUIET] Silence Can Never Condemn A Namespace Whose Silence Is The Design

- hardware-fact: `controller_main.cpp` gates throttle emission on a sample clock AND a deadband against the last EMITTED value, with its own comment reading "quiet-bus by construction". A stationary lever therefore publishes NOTHING, indefinitely, while being perfectly healthy.
- the-naive-rule-is-wrong: "no message in N seconds implies stale" marks the fleet's most-watched namespace DEAD seconds after David stops touching it. This is the same defect inverted — an instrument that condemns everything is exactly as useless as one that condemns nothing, and it passes precisely the same "did it go unknown?" test.
- rule: an EVENT_DRIVEN namespace has NO staleness timeout. `staleness_timeout` is `None` — not zero, not infinity, because the quantity is meaningless rather than extreme. Its own silence is evidence of nothing.
- what-does-condemn-it: the ABSENCE OF ITS NODE, and nothing else. This is what makes the per-node heartbeat load-bearing rather than belt-and-braces: for this entire class it is the only liveness signal that exists.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/namespace.py:Namespace.verdict
- verify: python/tests/test_freshness_receiver.py::test_a_quiet_by_construction_namespace_NEVER_expires_on_its_own_silence
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

### [CXC-FRESH-HEARTBEAT-RX] The Heartbeat Receiver — The Half That Was Never Built

- prior-state: `ComposeIOBus.publish_heartbeat` shipped and said so in its own docstring: "the receiver half — a monitor counting missed intervals and driving a node to degraded — does not exist anywhere in this repo, and spec/01 currently describes it as though it does." Beats went out, arrived, and were counted by nothing. HEARTBEAT messages reach neither the STATE nor the EVENT branch of the inbound dispatcher, so they fell through entirely.
- rule: a node is alive iff it has beaten within 3x the interval THE BEAT ITSELF DECLARED. The cadence rides in the payload (`interval_s`) precisely so the receiver never needs a configured second copy — the second copy being the thing that eventually disagrees.
- a-node-never-heard-from-is-not-alive: the tempting default is "no evidence of death", and it certifies every node in the fleet alive before a single beat arrives, including ones that never boot. Absence of evidence is not evidence of liveness.
- attribution: the receiver records which node published each namespace from the envelope's `src`, and that attribution is what routes a quiet namespace to its node's heartbeat. ⛔ NO ATTRIBUTED PUBLISHER MEANS NO VERDICT: a locally-published namespace, or one whose envelope carried no `src`, has no node to condemn, and condemning it anyway would mark every in-process namespace dead the moment this shipped. The heartbeat may only ever REMOVE confidence from a node positively identified and then positively lost.
- ordering: the freshness receiver sees every inbound message FIRST, before engine validation can reject a payload and return. A peer whose value violates our engine is still a peer that is ALIVE, and losing its attribution because its data was malformed would condemn it for the wrong reason.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/freshness.py:HeartbeatReceiver
- verify: python/tests/test_freshness_receiver.py::test_the_heartbeat_receiver_marks_a_node_silent_at_three_intervals
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

