## 4. `unknown` Is A Type, Not A Value

### [CXC-FRESH-UNKNOWN-TYPE] It Rides In The VALUE Domain So No Signature Has To Change

- rule: `unknown` is a singleton object delivered as the ordinary `value` argument. It is not a side-channel, not a second callback, not a flag beside the value.
- why: the widget seam is `on_bus_state(namespace, value)` and it is implemented 52 times. A freshness side-channel needs all 52 signatures changed, and until every one of them was, a widget would keep rendering a stale number while the receiver quietly knew better. Making unknown a member of the value domain is also simply what "type tag" MEANS — 0xFD sits beside 0xFE int32 and 0xFF float32, so its Python realization sits beside `int` and `float`.
- convergence: wire-unknown ("I am alive and I know that I do not know") and expiry-unknown (the receiver's own timeout) are DIFFERENT MECHANISMS REACHING ONE RECEIVER STATE. Both are required, because a dead publisher cannot send 0xFD.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/freshness.py:UNKNOWN
- verify: python/tests/test_freshness_receiver.py::test_wire_unknown_and_expiry_unknown_are_the_SAME_receiver_state
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

### [CXC-FRESH-UNKNOWN-ARITH] Arithmetic PROPAGATES; Coercion And Ordering REFUSE

- propagates: `UNKNOWN + 1` is UNKNOWN. This is [D4b]'s derived-value rule — "unknown if ANY input is unknown" — holding automatically wherever a binding transform does arithmetic, instead of every transform having to remember it.
- refuses: `int()`, `float()`, `<`, `>` raise `UnknownValueError`. `int(UNKNOWN) -> 0` would draw a zeroed gauge indistinguishable from a real zero, which is the exact lie this mechanism exists to stop, reintroduced one layer lower. `UNKNOWN > limit -> False` would SUPPRESS AN ALARM: a comparison that never ran reads identically to a threshold that was not crossed.
- equality-never-raises: `==` is identity-only and total, because dict lookups and the `new != old` change test run on every publish.
- engine-bypass: publishing UNKNOWN never touches the state engine. Unknown is a TYPE, not a member of any engine's domain, so validating it against one is a category error — and a dangerous one, because a `binary` engine handed an out-of-domain value raises inside a Tk callback, becomes an NSException on macOS, and aborts the process with SIGABRT. A publisher saying "I do not know" must never be able to kill the surface rendering it.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/freshness.py:_Unknown
- verify: python/tests/test_freshness_receiver.py::test_publishing_unknown_bypasses_the_state_engine
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

### [CXC-FRESH-WIRE] Tag 0xFD, And The Injectivity Defect It Nearly Shipped With

- encoding: `[0xFD]` with a ZERO-LENGTH payload, so the entire encoded value is one byte.
- the-residual-bites: 0xFE and 0xFF are safe because their discriminator is LENGTH — they are only ever READ as tags at five bytes, so the one-byte values 254 and 255 pass through the small-int fast path untouched. 0xFD HAS NO LENGTH TO BE DISCRIMINATED BY. Measured on the unmodified codec: `encode_value(253)` returned `b"\xfd"`, byte-identical to unknown, and `decode_value(b"\xfd")` returned integer 253.
- it-is-worse-than-mis-decoding: the failure is not that unknown would be read as 253. It is that THE CODEC STOPS BEING INJECTIVE — two distinct values share one encoding — and no care at the decoder can recover information the encoder threw away.
- fix: 253 is carved out of the small-int fast path and goes out TAGGED as int32. Four extra bytes for exactly one integer in the whole domain; 0..252 and 254..255 unchanged. Widening the carve-out to 254/255 would be cargo-culting a fix onto values that never collided.
- status: implemented
- implementation: python/composeio/transport_uart.py:VALUE_TAG_UNKNOWN
- verify: python/tests/test_value_codec_unknown_tag.py::test_the_codec_is_injective_across_every_single_byte_integer
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FRESHNESS]

